        "---\n",
        "title: \"A Replication of Karlan and List (2007)\"\n",
        "author: \"Mariana Vejar\"\n",
        "date: today\n",
        "callout-appearance: minimal # this hides the blue \"i\" icon on .callout-notes\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "In the economics of charitable giving, a central question is whether the \"price\" of giving affects donor behavior. Dean Karlan and John List designed a natural field experiment to explore this by mailing over 50,000 donation solicitations to previous contributors of a politically liberal nonprofit. The solicitations randomly varied the presence and size of a matching grant — where a \"leadership donor\" promised to match contributions at ratios of $1:$1, $2:$1, or $3:$1 — to see how this influenced the likelihood and size of donations.\n",
        "\n",
        "This intervention simulates a price change: for example, under a $2:$1 match, a $1 donation turns into $3 for the organization, lowering the \"price\" of public good provision from $1 to 33 cents. But does this lead people to give more?\n",
        "\n",
        "This blog post replicates some of Karlan and List’s main findings using their publicly shared data. We aim to explore how the match offer affected donation behavior, whether larger match ratios increased giving, and what this implies about human motivation in philanthropic settings.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data"
      ],
      "id": "f999dc3b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.read_stata(\"data/karlan_list_2007.dta\")\n",
        "df.head()"
      ],
      "id": "9444aeb3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
